$-\\frac{x}{2}\\le 4.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055021 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *divided* by $2.$ To isolate $x$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,4 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}) & \\ge -8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}2)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{x}{2}) & \\ge -8 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & \\ge -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & \\ge -8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & \\ge -8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\ge -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -8.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055261 1071 "Evaluate $1 + (2 - 3x)$ for $x =-1$." 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186055261 1071 "Evaluate $1+(2-3x)$ for $x=-1.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055261 1074 "We replace $x$ with $-1$ in the expression and evaluate the parentheses first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(2-3x) & = \\\\ 1+(2-3\\cdot (-1)) & = \\\\ 1+(2-(-3)) & = \\\\ 1+(2+3) & = \\\\ 1+5 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055516 1071 "Calculate $\\log_{9} (27)$ without using a calculator." 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186055516 1071 "Calculate ${log}_{9}⁡(27)$ without using a calculator." 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186055516 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9,$ and $X=27.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡(27) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(27)}{{log}_{3}⁡(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3})}{{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186064196 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536821 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536897 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186068893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536856 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186071330 1078 #edb/ref 17592186071335 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072621 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072622 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072623 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072624 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186072625 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536363 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536461 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536539 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536601 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536623 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186072620 1078 #edb/ref 17592186536623 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073590 1074 "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2)$.\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y)$.\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y)$.\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,3\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto (4,3-2)=(4, 1)\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-4,1\\right)\n$$\n\nunder the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1)$." 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186073590 1074 "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point $\\left(4,3\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto  (4,3-2)=(4, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $\\left(4,1\\right)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1) .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point $\\left(-4,1\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1).$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073591 1071 "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto (x,ky).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25),$ then what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073591 1071 "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y) \\mapsto (x,ky)$. If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25)$, then what is the value of $k$?" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186073591 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y ) \\mapsto (y, x) .\n$$\n\n \nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,14 ) \\mapsto (14,5) .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(14, 5) \\mapsto (14,  5k) .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25),$ by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k=25\\,⟹\\,k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073591 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$.\nApplying this to the point $P$, we get\n$(5,14) \\mapsto (14,5)$.\n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5)$, we get\n$(14, 5) \\mapsto (14, 5k)$.\n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25)$, by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5k = 25⟹k &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5$." 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186073650 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D$." 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186073650 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536848 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536876 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536889 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536815 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536860 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536870 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536839 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536869 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536843 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186073920 1050 900.0 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074182 1071 "The point $P(-5,-2)$ is rotated $270^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin $O$ and then translated according to the function $(x, y) ↦ (x + p, y + q)$. If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(2,-9)$, then $p + q =$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186074182 1071 "The point $P(-5,-2)$ is rotated ${270}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin $O$ and then translated according to the function $(x,y)↦(x+p,y+q).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(2,-9),$ then $p+q=$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074182 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${270}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(-5,-2)↦(-2,5).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-2,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-2,5)↦(-2+p,5+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(2,-9),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2+p=2 \\\\ 5+q=-9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}p=4 \\\\ q=-14.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np-q=4+(-14)=-10.\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074397 1074 "One of the points belonging to the graph is $(10,15).$\n\nLet's write the ratio $10:15$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10÷5}{15÷5} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio of lemon juice to water is $2:3.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186074980 1064 "We can combine transformations by applying them in turn.\n\nFor example, suppose that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ below is dilated with center $O$ and a scale factor $2$, and then shifted $3$ units down. What is the resulting segment?\n\n![](images/f7/f739b4363c116dcd1e1139a5111373e0f3f16557a7d4b9a58a0b12d0957dd4e4.png)\n\nLet's apply the transformations in turn.\n\n- Dilating with center $O$ and scale factor $2$ gives the segment $\\overline{A'B'}$, shown below:\n\n![](images/c0/c0a9c59e085f982d851d624cce09e37e27a6554e4de308f512246880d87c6ab6.png)\n\n- Next, we take $\\overline{A'B'}$ and shift it by $3$ units down. This gives the segment $\\overline{A''B''}$:\n\n![](images/a7/a78807d3f1fb43f3773671f7c4f5696dd3779ae9ce1d8da797377c3574170a92.png)" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186074980 1064 "We can combine transformations by applying them in turn.\n\nFor example, suppose that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ below is dilated with center $O$ and a scale factor $2,$ and then shifted $3$ units down. What is the resulting segment? \n\n![](images/bc/bc65e4edde561add5403cf57abc2cba50eabc4a9f7b8ba7592a