ing hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\sinh^2 x = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x & ={(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186168152 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\csch{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\csch^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186168152 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\csch{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186168153 1064 "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}.\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x.\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186168153 1064 "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} 1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186171231 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171236 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536850 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536865 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172017 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536881 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536893 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172601 1071 "![](images/32/32165a622d57d6af278b7ce288093ef4db6867619046b8625071f0274a1c7e93.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x^{3}y + x^{3}$\n2. $y^{′} = xy - x$\n3. $y^{′} = x^{2}y + x^{2}$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186172601 1071 "![](images/32/32165a622d57d6af278b7ce288093ef4db6867619046b8625071f0274a1c7e93.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186172601 1074 "![](images/a2/a278676af009b7096bd50f9b3918f0e8090c1822a9e029ac2b4b6325d1ba7a4c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}y+{x}^{3}={x}^{3}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\n evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173705 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173706 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173707 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173708 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173709 13194139535886 17592186045417 false] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536341 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536379 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536441 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186173704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186536624 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186173704 1075 #ed